--TEST--
Core\IO::writeStream lands its chunks in order as one file, and a second write to the same name refuses unless `overwrite` says otherwise — the default asserted by what is still on disk after the refusal rather than by the throw on its own — ADR 0105 § 4, spec § 14
--FILE--
<?nvs
// Three chunks, one file: what a caller gets is `write`'s result over a source
// it never had to join itself. The size is read back from the operating system
// rather than counted here, so a member that wrote a chunk twice or dropped one
// moves a number even where the text still looks plausible.
array<bytes> $chunks = ["one " as bytes, "two " as bytes, "three" as bytes];
Core\IO::writeStream("out.txt", $chunks);
echo "wrote: ", Core\IO::read("out.txt"), "\n";
echo "size: ", Core\IO::size("out.txt") as string, "\n";

// ADR 0105 § 4's first rule. The refusal is not the claim -- what is, is that
// the file still holds what the first call put there, which is the difference
// between refusing and truncating before refusing.
array<bytes> $second = ["replaced" as bytes];
try {
    Core\IO::writeStream("out.txt", $second);
    echo "the second write was allowed\n";
} catch (IOError $refused) {
    echo "refused: ", Core\IO::read("out.txt"), "\n";
}

// And with the rule turned off it replaces, which is what makes the refusal
// above a default rather than the only thing this member does.
Core\IO::writeStream("out.txt", $second, {overwrite: true});
echo "after overwrite: ", Core\IO::read("out.txt"), "\n";
--FILE nvs.toml--
[capabilities.fs]
read = ["."]
write = ["."]
--EXPECT--
wrote: one two three
size: 13
refused: one two three
after overwrite: replaced
